# Test-Driven Development

This project follows **Red → Green → Refactor** for behavior changes, with test design principles from [Angry Tests](https://github.com/yegor256/at).

## Mandatory Workflow

- Write a failing test first for bugs and new behavior in `src/lib/**`, `src/components/**`, and `src/pages/api/**`
- Implement the smallest change that makes the test pass
- Refactor only after the test suite is green
- Every bug must be reproduced by a test before being fixed
- Every new feature must be covered by a test before implementation
- Every time you change code, add more tests

## Test Pyramid

- Use **Vitest** for domain logic, helpers, and isolated React components — classify as "fast" (<100ms)
- Use **Playwright** for page flows, navigation, hydration, forms, and cross-route behavior — classify as "deep"
- Do not use E2E tests to cover logic that is better and faster expressed in unit tests
- Reproduce bugs with minimum scaffolding

## Test Design (Angry Tests)

### Basics

- **One assertion per test** — place it as the last statement
- Keep every test shorter than a dozen lines; aim for single-statement tests
- Name tests as full English sentences starting with a verb: `it('creates session cookie with valid credentials')`
- Never write comments inside test methods
- Assert in every test — no "setup-only" tests

### Isolation

- Never share mutable state between tests — no shared `beforeAll`/`beforeEach` with side effects
- Prepare clean state at the start of each test; don't clean up after
- Don't define constants (static literals) inside tests
- Don't inline magic constants; assign them to named variables first
- Code duplication in tests is the last problem to fix — clarity over DRY

### Inputs and Fixtures

- Use irregular, realistic inputs (non-ASCII, Unicode, edge cases) — no `"foo"`, `"bar"`, `"test"`
- Use different inputs in every test, don't reuse them
- Inline small fixtures instead of loading from files
- Don't keep large fixtures in static files; let tests generate them at runtime
- Don't call functions with default arguments in tests — provide explicit values

### What to Test

- Assert on the most vulnerable pain points
- Test public behavior, not implementation details
- Don't test getters, setters, or trivial functionality
- Don't test constructors if they only do assignments
- Don't assert on side effects like log output
- Don't assert on details of error messages — assert on error type or status
- Don't use mock framework verification (`.toHaveBeenCalled()`) when you can assert on output
- Favor fake objects and stubs over brittle mocks

### Quality

- Tests must pass without Internet; use ephemeral ports and timeouts
- Close resources after use (file handles, connections)
- Don't keep temporary files next to source code; use a temp directory
- Stop tests on timeout; don't let them run forever
- Don't sleep for arbitrary durations; wait for events instead
- Don't delete tests, disable/skip them instead (with a reason)
- Don't write production code that is only used by tests
- Parameterize tests when testing multiple inputs
- Let different tests test the same feature from different angles

## What to Cover First

- Domain invariants in `src/lib/**`
- Conditional rendering and interaction in reusable components
- Validation and failure modes in API handlers
- Security-sensitive behavior: auth cookies, origin checks, upload validation, rate limiting assumptions
- SEO-critical route behavior when page generation logic changes

## Validation Commands

- Smallest relevant unit run: `bun run test:run`
- Coverage when the change is broad or risky: `bun run test:coverage`
- Browser-level verification for route or UI flows: `bun run test:e2e`
- Static quality gate: `bun run lint`

## CI & Quality

- All CI workflows must pass before code review
- Use ESLint checks as a quality gate
- Use Playwright for E2E/browser testing

## Practical Guidance

- For purely editorial text changes, test only if behavior or rendering contracts can regress
- For layout-only changes, prefer targeted component coverage or Playwright checks over snapshot sprawl
- When fixing a production bug, keep the regression test as close as possible to the failure surface
